# Tutorial 29 — Accessibility, Settings and Controls
# Reference: docs/scripting/vns/runtime/vns-settings-modes.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_29
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A complete game gives players control over reading, sound, display, input, and motion. This workshop starts with a choice that changes the tutorial itself.
@label lesson_menu
> Use a still continue arrow -> still
> Use the spinning continue arrow -> animated
> Reading and audio preferences -> preferences
> Input, display and testing -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label still
[flag tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
Lavender: The continue arrow will stay still in the tutorial hub and lessons. You can restore its rotation here or from the hub.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label animated
[unflag tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
Lavender: The continue arrow spins again. Motion is a preference, not a requirement for understanding the lesson.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label preferences
Lavender: The runtime settings include text speed, auto delay, skip rules, channel volumes, UI font scale, accessibility themes, and text-to-speech support where the platform provides it.
Lavender: Try the largest font scale with long dialogue, speaker names, and choices. Keep essential information in text, and avoid conveying meaning only through color or sound.
Lavender: Auto-play and skip are different modes. Read-only skip protects unseen text; auto delay gives a line time after it is revealed. Test voiced and unvoiced lines separately.
Lavender: Preserve player choices. A story beat should not repeatedly reset volume or readability settings simply because it was revisited.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Test the full runtime using only the keyboard, then only the pointer. Remappable action bindings should remain coherent across dialogue, menus, history, and JES scenes.
Lavender: Check windowed and fullscreen modes, aspect ratio, small windows, long translations, muted audio, and missing speech support. Confirm that a player can always leave a modal interface.
Lavender: Exercise: finish three lessons with still drawings and large text, save, reload, and verify the experience remains understandable and controllable.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: A crucial clue is shown only by a flashing red effect. What should you add?
> A readable, non-motion way to understand it -> correct
> A stronger flash -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: More intensity does not make the information available to everyone. Give it a clear textual or static presentation too.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. The story should survive changes in how it is presented.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_29]
[flag tutorial_done_29]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_6]
